# The packages every Mica OS image carries, whatever board it is built for and
# whatever the build declines. Nothing here is selectable: a root with no
# mica-system has no state mounts, no seed and reconcile tools and no operator
# account, and a root with no mica-ca-trust cannot verify a TLS peer.
mica-system
mica-deploy
mica-ca-trust

# The SFTP server dropbear runs (/usr/lib/sftp-server), mica-core's package; every
# product installs it, no board selects it.
mica-sftp-server

# The emergency binary, and it is in `common` rather than a feature set on
# purpose. Every other manifest family is declinable -- a feature can be left
# out with --without, a radio with the board that has none -- and a tool an
# operator reaches for when the normal userland is damaged is worth nothing if
# the image in their hands is the build that declined it. It costs one 830 KB
# file, it enables nothing, nothing depends on it, and it changes no PATH; the
# whole of it is /usr/bin/busybox, reached by naming an applet.
mica-busybox

# Options of the Base floor (mica-system-base:docs/floor-and-options.md) every product keeps: the console login
# (without /usr/bin/login no getty starts) and the time zones. mica-tzdata is a package of the Base pool, login
# an upstream root of its lock. The operator tools beyond busybox are the feature `tools`, the web console the
# feature `ui` and the SSH server the feature `ssh`.
mica-tzdata
login
